================================================================================
Third-party components
================================================================================

The Apache-2.0 licence above covers Talkie's own source code. It does not, and
cannot, grant rights to the third-party software Talkie depends on. Three
things every build ships carry terms that anyone distributing a built
application has to meet: FFmpeg and eSpeak NG, which are GPL, and the FFmpeg
inside Electron, which is LGPL. eSpeak NG is also in this repository, compiled,
so its terms apply to sharing the source as well -- see its section below.

The full list of every dependency and its licence is generated from the
dependency tree and shown inside the app under Settings -> Licences. It is also
committed at src/renderer/src/generated/notices.json, and regenerated by
`npm run notices`.

Licence texts that no package carries -- the GPL, version 2 and version 3, the
LGPL, version 2.1, and ONNX Runtime's notices -- are kept in licences/ and
installed with every build at Talkie.app/Contents/Resources/licences/, where a
recipient can read them. Electron's and Chromium's are installed beside them,
in licences/electron/, copied from the installed electron package.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FFmpeg, x264, x265 -- GPL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Talkie records, exports and measures video with FFmpeg 9.0.2 as built by Martin Riedl
(https://ffmpeg.martin-riedl.de), configured --enable-gpl --enable-version3
with x264 and x265 linked in, which makes the binaries GPL-3.0-or-later.
`npm install` fetches them, pinned by SHA-256 in scripts/ffmpegBuilds.json,
into vendor/ffmpeg/. A packaged build carries them in
Contents/Resources/ffmpeg/.

None of these binaries is part of this repository.

This matters when binaries are distributed, not when source is:

  * Publishing or cloning THIS SOURCE REPOSITORY carries no GPL obligation,
    because no GPL code is contained in it.

  * Distributing a BUILT APPLICATION (a .dmg, a GitHub release asset, an app
    store submission) does distribute those GPL binaries. Whoever does that
    becomes a GPL distributor and must comply -- which includes offering the
    corresponding source of FFmpeg and its GPL dependencies to every recipient,
    and imposing no further restrictions on the rights the GPL grants them.

Talkie's own build is GPL-3.0-or-later, so a distributor may provide its source
from a network location. The corresponding source is the FFmpeg 9.0.2 release
plus the source of every library its `-buildconf` enables, because the build is
statically linked. x264 and x265 are what make it GPL. The others are not
covered by that choice: they carry their own licences, which a distributor of
the binary must also collect and carry. For the macOS arm64 build the
configuration names fontconfig, libaom, libass, libbluray, libdav1d,
libfreetype, libharfbuzz, libklvanc, libmp3lame, libopenh264, libopenjpeg,
libopus, librav1e, libsnappy, libsrt, libsvtav1, libtheora, libvmaf, libvorbis,
libvpx, libvvenc, libwebp, libx264, libx265, libxml2, libzimg, libzvbi and
openssl. Their licences have not been collected here. `ffmpeg -buildconf` on
vendor/ffmpeg/<system>/ffmpeg prints the exact configuration of each build.

The links below are upstream release pages, not corresponding source. The GPL
asks for the source of the binary actually shipped -- FFmpeg 9.0.2 as it was
configured and built, with its exact flags -- so a distributor has to publish
that tree, or a tarball of it, beside the download.

FFmpeg source:  https://ffmpeg.org/download.html
x264 source:    https://www.videolan.org/developers/x264.html
x265 source:    https://www.videolan.org/developers/x265.html

x264 and x265 are themselves GPL-2.0-or-later; their copy of that licence is in
licences/GPL-2.0.txt. Linked into a GPL-3.0-or-later build, they are distributed
under version 3, which is what allows source from a network location.

Talkie itself does not use the GPL encoders. Recording and export both ask for
h264_videotoolbox, the hardware encoder built into every Mac Talkie runs on.
That does not make the shipped binaries any less GPL -- they were linked with
x264 and x265 and stay covered however they are invoked -- but it means no
feature depends on them, and a build of FFmpeg without --enable-gpl would serve
Talkie equally, removing these obligations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Electron and Chromium -- the application itself
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Talkie is an Electron application, so every build ships Electron (MIT) and the
Chromium it is built on: about 780 components under their own licences -- BSD,
MIT, Apache-2.0, LGPL, MPL and others. Their texts are installed with every
build at Talkie.app/Contents/Resources/licences/electron/ -- Electron's LICENSE
and Chromium's LICENSES.chromium.html. electron-builder does not carry those
two files into a macOS app by itself, so electron-builder.yml copies them from
the installed electron package, the version it packages.

One of those components carries a source duty, like the GPL ones above: the
FFmpeg Chromium plays audio and video with (libffmpeg.dylib) is
LGPL-2.1-or-later. It is a different build from the FFmpeg Talkie runs itself.
A distributor must offer its source -- Chromium's third_party/ffmpeg at the
revision the shipped Electron was built from. It ships as a separate library
Talkie does not modify, which keeps it replaceable, as the LGPL asks. Its
licence text is in licences/LGPL-2.1.txt.

Electron's FFmpeg source:  https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/third_party/ffmpeg

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
eSpeak NG -- GPL-3.0-or-later, inside the Kokoro voice
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Kokoro voice turns text into phonemes with eSpeak NG, licensed under the
GNU General Public License, version 3 or later. It reaches Kokoro through the
`phonemizer` package -- not the one on npm, which ships an eSpeak NG nobody can
name the source of, but Talkie's own build of it in packages/phonemizer: the
same Apache-2.0 wrapper around eSpeak NG compiled to JavaScript from a pinned
commit. The root package.json overrides `phonemizer` with it, so kokoro-js
loads that one.

Talkie keeps it at arm's length. kokoro-js, phonemizer, transformers.js and ONNX
Runtime are loaded only by a separate worker program (out/main/kokoroWorker.cjs)
that the app starts as its own process and exchanges plain messages with -- the
same footing as the GPL FFmpeg above. Nothing in the main process, the windows
or the MCP server imports them; src/main/tts/kokoro/kokoroBoundary.test.ts fails
the build if anything does.

Unlike FFmpeg, this compiled eSpeak NG is in this repository
(packages/phonemizer/src/espeakng.worker.js and dist/phonemizer.cjs), so the
GPL binds whoever distributes the repository as well as whoever distributes a
built application. Its corresponding source is:

  eSpeak NG at commit b774a8fd712e2b43510c1cb6c2b8cd3f22fe0a86
  https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng/archive/b774a8fd712e2b43510c1cb6c2b8cd3f22fe0a86.tar.gz
  SHA-256 e080573e4e0f60c52b7ca9963a8ea52560500e47190bccf795f23986c299bdfd

plus the two patches, the build script and the instructions in
packages/phonemizer/build/ and packages/phonemizer/BUILD.md, which travel with
every copy -- in a built application, in its node_modules/phonemizer.

That archive is GitHub's, served for eSpeak NG's repository, and could go if
that repository changed. So Talkie keeps its own copy, byte for byte the same,
attached to a release of this repository:

  https://github.com/murthy-chelankuri/talkie/releases/download/espeak-ng-source-b774a8f/espeak-ng-b774a8fd712e2b43510c1cb6c2b8cd3f22fe0a86.tar.gz

It stays there for as long as Talkie distributes this build. Anyone else
distributing either form takes on the same duty: point recipients at that
copy, or keep one and offer it themselves.

eSpeak NG source:  https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng

The Kokoro model weights themselves (hexgrad/Kokoro-82M, converted to ONNX by
onnx-community) are Apache-2.0. They are not shipped in the app: Talkie
downloads them from Hugging Face the first time Kokoro is chosen, pinned to one
revision and checked against its published SHA-256.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Other notable licences
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  * MIT, with third-party notices: ONNX Runtime (onnxruntime-node), which runs
    the Kokoro voice. Its npm package ships no licence file, so its MIT notice
    and ThirdPartyNotices.txt -- the components compiled into its native
    library, among them Eigen (MPL-2.0), protobuf and abseil -- are kept in
    licences/onnxruntime/ and installed with the app.

  * sharp is NOT shipped. transformers.js depends on it for image processing,
    which Kokoro never does, and the real package brings libvips: one library
    holding about thirty others, eight of them LGPL-3.0, cairo under MPL-2.0
    and libaom under a patent licence. package.json overrides it with a stub
    (stubs/sharp) so none of that is installed or distributed. See
    stubs/sharp/README.md before changing that.

  * EPL-2.0 (file-level copyleft): source for the covered files must be made
    available. Affects elkjs. Unmodified, this is satisfied by pointing at
    upstream.

  * dompurify is "(MPL-2.0 OR Apache-2.0)" -- a choice, not a requirement.
    Talkie elects Apache-2.0, under which nothing further is owed.
  * OFL-1.1: the Geist and Newsreader fonts are embedded in the app. They may
    not be sold on their own, and their copyright notices must travel with them.

Everything else in the dependency tree is MIT, ISC, Apache-2.0, BSD or similarly
permissive, and asks only that its copyright notice be reproduced -- which the
in-app Licences screen does.
